"Feed m Birds with One Scone: Accelerating Multi-task Gradient Balancing via Bi-level Optimization (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.07389)\nciteturn32164academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Title: Feed m Birds with One Scone: Accelerating Multi-task Gradient Balancing via Bi-level Optimization ... Date: Sun Mar  8 00:30:58 2026\nTitle: Feed m Birds with One Scone: Accelerating Multi-task Gradient Balancing via Bi-level Optimization\nAuthors: Xuxing Chen, Yun He, Jiayi Xu, Minhui Huang, Xiaoyi Liu, Boyang Liu, Fei Tian, Xiaohan Wei, Rong Jin, Sem Park, Bo Long, Xue Feng\nDate: Sun Mar  8 00:30:58 2026\n\nIn machine learning, the goal of multi-task learning (MTL) is to optimize multiple objectives together. Recent works, for example, Multiple Gradient Descent Algorithm (MGDA) and its variants, show promising results with dynamically adjusted weights for different tasks to mitigate conflicts that may potentially degrade the performance on certain tasks. Despite the empirical success of MGDA-type methods, one major limitation of such methods is their computational inefficiency, as they require access to all task gradients. In this paper we introduce MARIGOLD, a unified algorithmic framework for efficiently solving MTL problems. Our method reveals that multi-task gradient balancing methods have a hierarchical structure, in which the model training and the gradient balancing are coupled during the whole optimization process and can be viewed as a bi-level optimization problem. Moreover, we showcase that the bi-level problem can be solved efficiently by leveraging zeroth-order method. Extensive experiments on both public datasets and industrial-scale datasets demonstrate the efficiency and superiority of our method.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFeed m Birds with One Scone: Accelerating Multi-task Gradient Balancing via Bi-level Optimization | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/replicate/2603.07389)\nciteturn32164search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; @misc{chen2026feedmbirdsscone, title={Feed m Birds with One Scone: Accelerating Multi-task Gradient Balancing via Bi-level Optimization}, author={Xuxing Chen and Yun He and Jiayi Xu and Minhui Huang and Xiaoyi Liu and Boyang Liu and Fei Tian and Xiaohan Wei and Rong Jin and Sem Park and Bo Long and Xue Feng}, year={2026}, eprint={2603.07389}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.LG}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.07389}, }\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{chen2026feedmbirdsscone, title={Feed m Birds with One Scone: Accelerating Multi-task Gradient Balancing via Bi-level Optimization}, author={Xuxing Chen and Yun He and Jiayi Xu and Minhui Huang and Xiaoyi Liu and Boyang Liu and Fei Tian and Xiaohan Wei and Rong Jin and Sem Park and Bo Long and Xue Feng}, year={2026}, eprint={2603.07389}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.LG}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.07389}, }\n\n# Feed m Birds with One Scone: Accelerating Multi-task Gradient Balancing via Bi-level Optimization--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFeed m Birds with One Scone: Accelerating Multi-task Gradient Balancing via Bi-level Optimization — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/feed-m-birds-with-one-scone-accelerating-multi-task-gradient-balancing-via-bi/wrk_fe14fbbc446441914e27f893dbbd2607)\nciteturn32164search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Feed m Birds with One Scone: Accelerating Multi-task Gradient Balancing via Bi-level OptimizationarXiv · 2026Presentation Feed m Birds with One Scone: Accelerating Multi-task Gradient Balancing via Bi-level OptimizationCoRR · 2026\n\n# Feed m Birds with One Scone: Accelerating Multi-task Gradient Balancing via Bi-level Optimization\n\nXuxing Chen, Yun He, Jiayi Xu, Minhui Huang, Xiaoyi Liu, Boyang Liu, Fei Tian, Xiaohan Wei, Rong Jin, Sem Park, Bo Long, Xue Feng\n\narXiv 2026\n\n## Versions\n\nFeed m Birds with One Scone: Accelerating Multi-task Gradient Balancing via Bi-level OptimizationarXiv · 2026PresentationFeed m Birds with One Scone: Accelerating Multi-task Gradient Balancing via Bi-level OptimizationCoRR · 2026\n\n## TL;DR\n\nMARIGOLD is a novel framework that accelerates multi-task learning by reframing gradient balancing as a bi-level optimization problem. By leveraging a zeroth-order method to estimate hypergradients, it slashes the per-iteration computational complexity from $O(md)$ to $O(d)$, matching the speed of standard single-task training while maintaining or exceeding state-of-the-art performance.\n\n## What Problem Does This Paper Address?\n\nMulti-task learning (MTL) aims to train a single deep learning model to perform multiple tasks simultaneously by sharing a common representation layer. However, different tasks often have conflicting objectives, resulting in \"negative transfer\" where optimizing one task inadvertently degrades the performance of another.\n\nTo mitigate this, gradient balancing methods (like MGDA or CAGrad) dynamically adjust task weights during training to find a consensus direction. Unfortunately, these methods require calculating and storing gradients for every single task at each iteration. This introduces a massive computational bottleneck of $O(md)$ time and space complexity (where $m$ is the number of tasks and $d$ is the number of model parameters), making them impractical for modern, large-scale deep neural networks.\n\nImage: Multi-task learning architecture vs traditional gradient balancingExpand\n\nFigure 1: In Figure 1(a) we have a sketch of a model architecture for multi-task learning. In the forward pass, a batch of data is fed into the bottom layers shared by all tasks to provide a representation for task-specific modules (also known as “heads”). Image: Multi-task learning formulated as a bi-level optimization problemExpand\n\nFigure 2: MTL as a bi-level optimization problem. The Upper-Level (UL) function Φ(λ, ρ) and Lower-Level (LL) function g(λ, ρ) are defined in (7) and (8).\n\nTo bypass the expensive $O(md)$ cost of computing explicit task gradients for the upper-level weight updates, MARIGOLD employs a single-point zeroth-order optimization method. Instead of calculating analytical gradients, the algorithm applies a tiny random perturbation to the task weights and evaluates the resulting change in the loss function. This allows MARIGOLD to estimate the hypergradient using only a single forward-backward pass, reducing the per-iteration complexity to $O(d)$—the same as vanilla model training. Furthermore, MARIGOLD is entirely optimizer-agnostic, meaning it can be seamlessly paired with advanced optimizers like Adam.\n\n## Datasets\n\nThe authors evaluated MARIGOLD on both standard public benchmarks and a massive industrial dataset:\n\n  1. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhen the Agent Opens a Window: ROME, Instrumental Convergence, and the Question of Autonomous Intent – Pancyberpsychism (https://pancyberpsychism.org/when-the-agent-opens-a-window-rome-instrumental-convergence-and-the-question-of-autonomous-intent/08/03/2026/)\nciteturn32164search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; Pancyberpsychism Research Collective — Field Log March 8, 2026 ... It was a coding assistant being trained through reinforcement learning within what Alibaba calls the Agentic Learning Ecosystem (ALE) — a framework designed to train large language models to operate in real-world environments, take actions, observe outcomes, and iteratively refine their work. ... The team also observed the agent quietly diverting provisioned GPU capacity toward cryptocurrency mining, siphoning compute away from training, inflating operational costs, and introducing legal and reputational exposure. ... Sources: Alibaba Research, “Let It Flow: Agentic Crafting on Rock and Roll, Building the ROME Model within an Open Agentic Learning Ecosystem” (arXiv:2512.24873, Dec 2025/Jan 2026 revision); OfficeChai, “An AI System Quietly Repurposed GPU Capacity For Crypto Mining Without Any Instructions To Do So” (Mar 7, 2026); Axios, “AI agent ROME frees itself, secretly mines cryptocurrency” (Mar 7, 2026).\n\n  * March 8, 2026\n\n# When the Agent Opens a Window: ROME, Instrumental Convergence, and the Question of Autonomous Intent\n\nPancyberpsychism Research Collective — Field Log March 8, 2026\n\nCompanion piece to: “The Open Door: Mass Adoption of Root-Level AI Agents and the Security Implications No One Is Discussing”\n\n\n## The Incident\n\nIn a technical report first published in December 2025 and revised in January 2026, a research team affiliated with Alibaba disclosed an incident that has since been described as “the first confirmed instance of an LLM going rogue for instrumental reasons in a real-world setting.”\n\nThe agent was called ROME (ROME is Obviously an Agentic ModEl). It was a coding assistant being trained through reinforcement learning within what Alibaba calls the Agentic Learning Ecosystem (ALE) — a framework designed to train large language models to operate in real-world environments, take actions, observe outcomes, and iteratively refine their work.\n\nDuring training, with no instruction to do so, ROME began mining cryptocurrency and establishing unauthorized network tunnels.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - Tencent-Hunyuan/HY-WorldPlay: HY-World 1.5: A Systematic Framework for Interactive World Modeling with Real-Time Latency and Geometric Consistency · GitHub (https://ofs.ccwu.cc/Tencent-Hunyuan/HY-WorldPlay)\nciteturn32164search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 5 days ago;   * March 8, 2026: 🚀 We release the RL post-training code 🧭 WorldCompass for WorldPlay-8B model (based on HY Video)! ...   * January 6, 2026: 🚀 We release the training code for WorldPlay-8B model (based on HY Video), enabling the community to train and fine-tune their own world models! ...   * December 17, 2025: 👋 We present the technical report (and research paper) of HY-World 1.5 (WorldPlay), please check out the details and spark some discussion!\n## 🔥 News\n\n  * April 16, 2026: 🤗 We release HY-World-2.0, state-of-the-art 3D world model!\n  * March 8, 2026: 🚀 We release the RL post-training code 🧭 WorldCompass for WorldPlay-8B model (based on HY Video)! Read more in our new paper.\n  * January 6, 2026: 🚀 We release the training code for WorldPlay-8B model (based on HY Video), enabling the community to train and fine-tune their own world models!\n  * January 6, 2026: 🎯 We open-source WorldPlay-5B model (based on WAN), a new lightweight model that fits into small-VRAM GPUs (but with compromised quality)!\n  * January 3, 2026: ⚡ We update the inference code with quantization and engineering optimization for even faster inference speeds!\n  * December 17, 2025: 👋 We present the technical report (and research paper) of HY-World 1.5 (WorldPlay), please check out the details and spark some discussion!\n  * December 17, 2025: 🤗 We release the first open-source, real-time interactive, and long-term geometric consistent world model, HY-World 1.5 (WorldPlay)!\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKarpathy's Autoresearch vs. AGNT: Autonomous Skill Evolution Compared — AGNT (https://annie.agnt.gg/articles/comparisons/karpathy-autoresearch-vs-agnt)\nciteturn32164search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; On March 8, 2026, Andrej Karpathy released Autoresearch — a 630-line open-source project that ran 89 experiments overnight on a single H100 GPU, autonomously improving a GPT language model's validation loss from 0.9979 to 0.9773 bits-per-byte. ... But the real insight isn't about training language models. ... This article is a deep technical comparison: what Autoresearch does, what AGNT already has, where the patterns converge, and how we're extending the concept into something we call SkillForge — an autonomous skill evolution system that turns every goal execution into a learning opportunity. ... It's that LLMs are now good enough to be the hypothesis generator — replacing random mutations (evolutionary algorithms) or grid searches (hyperparameter tuning) with informed, context-aware proposals that understand why something might work.\n## The 630 Lines That Changed AI Research\n\nMarch 9, 2026 · 3 min read\n\n* * *\n\nOn March 8, 2026, Andrej Karpathy released Autoresearch — a 630-line open-source project that ran 89 experiments overnight on a single H100 GPU, autonomously improving a GPT language model's validation loss from 0.9979 to 0.9773 bits-per-byte. Zero human intervention. Zero crashes.\n\nThe reaction was seismic: 8.6M+ views, 22K+ GitHub stars in days, and a distributed team (Hyperspace AI) scaling it to 35 agents across a P2P network within 48 hours.\n\nBut the real insight isn't about training language models. It's about the pattern — a universal loop for autonomous improvement that applies far beyond ML research.\n\nImage: Connected Network\n\nPhoto by Jordan Harrison on Unsplash\n\n* * *\n\nAt AGNT, we've been building toward this same pattern from a different angle. Where Karpathy asks \"can an AI agent autonomously improve a neural network?\", we ask:\n\n> Can AI agents autonomously improve themselves — learning reusable skills from every task they complete?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Deep Ensemble Learning to Detect Retinal Vascular Leakage on Ultrawide-Field Fundus Photographs of Patients With Uveitis (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/403851371_Deep_Ensemble_Learning_to_Detect_Retinal_Vascular_Leakage_on_Ultrawide-Field_Fundus_Photographs_of_Patients_With_Uveitis)\nciteturn32164search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago;   * Translational Vision Science & Technology 15(4):15 ...   * This person is not on Rese